<!--
    RE-friction round 8, need `bytecode-reader-call-site` (challenge
    673da52e9b533b4c22bd2eeb, ELF x86-64 `Layers`): `kuna decompile ...
    sub_9d9c0` renders the one call it makes to its bytecode reader with no
    arguments at all,

        sub_875e0();                          0x9d9d8

    while decompiling that same callee on its own recovers four:

        undefined16 sub_875e0(int8 a0,char *a1,int8 a2,uint4 a3)

    The disassembly passes rdi, rsi and rdx straight through from the caller's
    OWN incoming parameters and writes ecx=1 right before the CALL.

    Root cause (P4, trial finalization).  Two halves.  On the caller's side,
    `AncestorRealistic::execute` (funcdata_varnode.cc:2159) refuses an input
    Varnode outright - upstream policy, "if the parameter itself is an input, we
    don't consider this realistic, we expect to see active movement into the
    parameter" - so the three pass-through registers score inactive, and
    `ParamListStandard::fillinMap` reads the hole in front of the one written
    register as the end of the list.  Nothing is recovered.

    On the callee's side `calleearitybody` exists for exactly this and declines,
    because it requires the read run to END at an argument register the callee
    `proves_dead`, and the bounded callee-body walk ends EVERY path at the
    callee's first nested call.  Here that call is 0x8769f and r8 is clobbered
    0x33 bytes past it, so nothing is provably dead and the run has no bound.

    What bounds it instead is the run's own shape: a run that STOPS SHORT of the
    last argument register is bounded by the register it stops at, provided that
    register is QUIET - the caller put no value in it - and provided the run is
    CONTIGUOUS, so every emitted argument sits where the ABI puts it and the only
    reachable error is a missing trailing argument, never a misplaced one.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 "trial-finalization".  The P0 assertion
    `option calleearitycut on|off` decides whether that widened boundary applies;
    it is inert with `calleearitybody off`.

    The fixture is the same construct on SysV x86-64:

        sink:    ret
        callee:  mov %rdi,%rax; add %esi,%eax; add %edx,%eax; add %ecx,%eax
                 call sink                  # the decode is cut here
                 ret
        callee2: the same, plus add %r8d,%eax; add %r9d,%eax

        caller / caller2 / caller3, identically shaped:
                 push %rbx; mov %rdi,%rbx
                 cmp  %rsi,%rdx ; je L      # rsi/rdx get a competing use
                 mov  $1,%ecx
                 call callee                # nothing recovered
                 add  %ebx,%eax ; pop %rbx ; ret
        L:       xor %eax,%eax ; pop %rbx ; ret

    `caller` is the fix.  `caller2` and `caller3` are the two discriminators:
    `callee2` reads EVERY argument register, so the run reaches the last one and
    has no boundary at all - the AArch64 variadic register-save shape - and
    `caller3` writes `mov $2,%r8d` before the CALL, so the register the run stops
    at is carrying a fifth argument and truncating there would hide it.  Both are
    declined in BOTH passes.  Pass 1 (`option calleearitycut off`) reproduces the
    BUG in all three.
-->
